---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Senj, 1943"
  eyebrow="7–8 October 1943"
  dek="Half the town was destroyed in two days. Hedviga was seventeen and living in it, and her aunt lost a husband and two daughters that year.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This archive had Senj as an economic story — <a href={u("/senj")}>a port that
    the railway killed in 1873</a>. It is also a war story, and the war reached the family directly.
    <strong>Nothing in the family tree says so</strong>; the dates do.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What happened</h2>
    <p>Italy capitulated on <strong>8 September 1943</strong>, and the Italian occupation zone along
    the Adriatic collapsed. Senj, with its harbour and its road up to Lika, became
    <strong>one of the most important Partisan ports</strong> on the coast: weapons and war material
    were concentrated in the town and ships lay in the harbour.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">On <strong>7 and 8 October 1943</strong> German aircraft bombed the
    town and the port.</p>
    <div class="grid three" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">52</div><div class="l">killed — <strong>30 local
        residents</strong> and 22 foreigners</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">~60</div><div class="l">civilians recorded seriously or
        lightly wounded</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">50%</div><div class="l">of the town destroyed to its
        foundations; only about a quarter of buildings still habitable</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Figures from the Croatian scholarly literature on the
    German bombing of Senj in 1943. <a href={u("/sources")}>Sources →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The family in it</div>
    <h2>Anka Blažević's household</h2>
    <p class="lede"><a href={u("/people/anka-blazevic")}><strong>Anka Blažević</strong></a>
    (1893–1953) was <a href={u("/people/ljubomir-blazevic")}>Ljubomir's</a> sister — Hedviga's aunt,
    and the <em>radnica</em> who worked the floor of the
    <a href={u("/senj")}>tobacco factory</a> alongside her sister-in-law
    <a href={u("/milka")}>Milka</a>. On <strong>20 November 1919</strong> she married
    <strong>Pavao Kosina</strong>, born at Senj on 15 March 1894.</p>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <tr><th>Pavao Kosina</th><td>b. 15 March 1894, Senj — <strong>died 8 October 1943</strong>,
        the second day of the bombing</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Otilija Kosina</th><td>b. 1920 — <strong>died 1943</strong>, no day recorded</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Antun Kosina</th><td>b. 1921 — died 2003. <strong>A priest</strong>: his cross reads
        <em>vlč.</em> — <em>velečasni</em>, Reverend</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Oskar Kosina</th><td>b. 4 September 1922, Senj — died <strong>1954, Buenos
        Aires</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Neda Kosina</th><td>b. 1924 — <strong>died 7 September 1943</strong>, a month before
        the raid</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Križan Kosina</th><td>b. 1940 — died 1941, an infant</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem">Anka outlived them by a decade and died in 1953.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What is claimed, and what is not</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Documented</th><td>German aircraft bombed Senj on <strong>7–8 October 1943</strong>,
        killing 30 local residents and 22 others and destroying about half the town.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Documented<br /><span class="dim">in the tree</span></th><td>Pavao Kosina died on
        <strong>8 October 1943</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Inferred</th><td>That he was <strong>one of the thirty</strong>. A man of forty-nine
        dying at Senj on the exact day of the second raid is a strong coincidence — but the date
        comes from the family tree, not from a death entry seen here, and this archive has not read
        the 1943 Senj register.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">Not claimed</th><td>That <strong>Otilija</strong> (1943, undated) or
        <strong>Neda</strong> (<strong>7 September</strong> 1943) died in the bombing.
        Neda's date is <em>a month before it</em> — and the day before Italy's capitulation. Three
        deaths in one household in one year is not one event until something says it is.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>What would settle it.</strong> The <strong>Senj death register for
    1943</strong>, which would give cause and place for all three; and the published list of the
    town's bombing victims, which exists in the local historical literature.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Where it leaves the rest of the family</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Hedviga was seventeen</h3>
        <p>She was born in Senj in 1926 and her children were born there and at Crikvenica in 1948,
        1951 and 1953 — so she was in the town, or near it, when half of it came down. Her brother
        <a href={u("/oto")}>Oto</a> was twenty, and had <strong>two years to live</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">The <a href={u("/hedviga")}>first version of her page here</a>
        said she was “fifteen when the war reached the coast”. It reached her street.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>And then Argentina</h3>
        <p>Her cousin <strong>Oskar Kosina</strong> (1922–1954) died at
        <strong>Buenos Aires</strong>. That is the second documented destination of this family
        after <a href={u("/port-pirie")}>Port Pirie</a> — and it matches the surname statistics
        exactly: of the sixty-nine people in the world spelling the name
        <strong><em>Zubrinic</em></strong>, <strong>twenty-nine are in Argentina</strong>, the
        largest group anywhere. <a href={u("/name")}>The names →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
